<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xml:lang="EN" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Neural Transplant Plast</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">NP</journal-id><journal-title>Journal of Neural Transplantation &#x00026; Plasticity</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0792-8483</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">1782256</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2565098</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1155/NP.1991.243</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>New Surgical Approach To Overcome The Inability Of InjuredMammalian Axons To Grow Within Their Environment</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Solomon</surname><given-names>Arieh</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"><sup>1</sup></xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Lavie</surname><given-names>Vered</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I2"><sup>2</sup></xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Ben-Bassat</surname><given-names>Sjana</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"><sup>1</sup></xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Belkin</surname><given-names>Michael</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"><sup>1</sup></xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Schwartz</surname><given-names>Michal</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I2"><sup>2</sup></xref><xref ref-type="corresp" rid="cor1">*</xref></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="I1"><sup>1</sup>Goldschleger Eye Research Institute, Sackler School of Medicine, Tel Aviv University, Tel Hashomer, Israel</aff><aff id="I2"><sup>2</sup>Department of Neurobiology, The Weizmann Institute of Science, Rehovot 76100, Israel</aff><author-notes><corresp id="cor1">*Michal Schwartz</corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><year>1991</year></pub-date><volume>2</volume><issue>3-4</issue><fpage>243</fpage><lpage>248</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1991.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>1991</copyright-year></permissions><abstract><p>We present a new method for creatingconditions conducive to axonal growth ininjured optic nerves of adult rabbits. Thesurgical approach consists of making a cavity inthe adult rabbit optic nerve, into which a piece ofnitrocellulose soaked with conditioned mediumoriginating from regenerating fish optic nervesis implanted. In addition, daily irradiation (10days, 5 min, 35 mW) with low energy He-Nelaser is carried out. Such a combined treatmentmay open a door to neurobiologists andclinicians, hoping to unravel the enigma ofmammalian CNS regeneration.</p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>